#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "a38dad3a-1afd-5e97-9046-87e413574865" :question/math-academy-id "e-21642" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-8x(t); y^{′}(t) =-8y(t)$\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-8\n$$\n\nwith the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $e^{-8t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$, so the origin is a stable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/24987f26ca917591ec4d8c2ddbbf8735.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2c335ee-467a-5114-a7d9-4c2692562d9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "e3e083fd-2421-5f66-acb5-69e1523e7fb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21643" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) - 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A$. The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}$. Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$, the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}te^{-2t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}) \\\\\n&= c_{1}e^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}e^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t + 1\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-2t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/40daf39b4da4ba08b07408a1a52d58e0.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t + 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhen $c_2=1$, we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{x = te^{-2t}; y = (-t + 1)e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/93a4716b66152e1696615a978d123f9f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/c14ad78173ebf05ed8f6540484bba45a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166479 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "4aa5583f-003b-55a7-bfac-ae42ac44a380" :question/math-academy-id "e-21645" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 6x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) + 4y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system." :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/3f407fe5a2055332f131e6ddc696a3a8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2c335ee-467a-5114-a7d9-4c2692562d9e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862a45ff-157e-5e07-8993-e016d7815b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[1; t]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164324} {:answer/id #uuid "048e8bd0-e114-5200-9333-03192372cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[t; 1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164325} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfd15e7-35f6-5121-957d-4b7f127b2b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[2; 2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164326} {:answer/id #uuid "5860ebeb-5c92-5975-b006-bc2c454f41ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[2t; 1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164327}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4afd746c-4997-523d-a177-3fb385e16ee9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164323} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90bf21f8-bd92-5c33-88df-3d6df764e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164329} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4ffc80-aa25-57d0-82d7-d3a072fe4d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{− t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164330} {:answer/id #uuid "21b84d7d-c2be-5618-bf97-050ffd89cc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164331} {:answer/id #uuid "3133c81d-2acd-5657-8370-ff3927fbaec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{− t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164332}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f324a1d2-9839-557a-82ec-dd9d81d4c710" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164328}] :question/id #uuid "5510f723-c114-5fc6-897e-1de8385b1619" :question/math-academy-id "q-332743" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 5y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v = [2, 2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $λ = 3$ of the system's matrix $A$. The vector $w = [- 1, 0]^{T}$ is such that $(A - λI)w = v$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$e^{-t}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3e1f462-d9f5-5b7a-ba67-0df925d0795e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "degenerate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164477} {:answer/id #uuid "c82cd374-d831-5577-8309-b43ff211155b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "star" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164478}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f4771df1-fcf4-5891-af11-d14d3749a57d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164476} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d356d83-56c3-5bb3-a7e5-fd0f681592f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164480} {:answer/id #uuid "4dca333c-139a-5157-a95b-bb52e7de0c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164481}] :answer-field/id #uuid "46f3a3ea-894a-514e-b220-702286b032fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164479}] :question/id #uuid "b699002a-13ee-52d0-9a5f-852554c5f030" :question/math-academy-id "q-333672" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t); y^{′}(t) = y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system. The equilibrium point (origin) is a\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnode."}]}
